NNAAMMEE
       pamtilt - print the tilt angle of a PGM file


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppaammttiilltt  [--aannggllee==_m_a_x_a_n_g_l_e]  [--ffaasstt]  [--qquuaalliittyy==_q] [--hhsstteepp==_n] [--vvsstteepp==_n]
       [--ddsstteepp==_n] [--aasstteepp==_n] [--vveerrbboossee] [_p_g_m_f_i_l_e]


EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
           scanimage --mode Gray --resolution 300 >crooked.pgm
           pnmrotate -b white `pamtilt crooked.pgm` crooked.pgm >straight.pgm
           (then crop, threshold, etc.)



DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppaammttiilltt tries to find the correct angle for  untilting  (de-skewing)  a
       scanned  text  document.   The output is a single floating-point number
       (the angle in degrees) for use as the argument to pnmrotate.

       "Document skew" is the name given to what happens when you feed a  page
       into  an  image  scanner  at  an  angle: the resulting image is tilted.
       ppaammttiilltt aims to help correct that.

       ppaammttiilltt makes three iterations at successively finer increments,  test‐
       ing  prospective  rotation  angles to find the best one.  ppaammttiilltt works
       best for straightening images with strong  horizontal  lines  and  does
       poorly  with arbitrary photos.  If ppaammttiilltt has no confidence in its re‐
       sults, it prints the special value 00.00; you can  check  for  this  or
       just pass it as a legal argument to pnmrotate.

       ppaammttiilltt operates on the first plane of the input image, which is either
       PNM or PAM, and ignores any other planes.  Ordinarily, the input is PGM
       or GRAYSCALE PAM, so there is only one plane.

       ppaammttiilltt  works  on  bilevel  (PBM, BLACKANDWHITE PAM) images as well as
       grayscale, but you will minimize artifacts if you scan  and  rotate  in
       grayscale before you apply a threshold to make a bilevel image.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ),  ppaammttiilltt  recognizes  the
       following command line options:




       --aannggllee==_m_a_x_a_n_g_l_e
              Assume  a  maximum tilt angle of _m_a_x_a_n_g_l_e (measured in degrees).
              The default value is sufficient  for  most  images,  even  those
              scanned somewhat carelessly.

              The default is 10.0.


       --ffaasstt  Skip the third iteration for speed at the expense of accuracy.


       --vveerrbboossee
              Show  on Standard Error the measurements computed at each tested
              angle.



       Here are some other options you  can  use  to  tune  the  operation  of
       ppaammttiilltt  but  they're  seldom needed.  The default values accommodate a
       wide variety of input documents.




       --qquuaalliittyy==_q
              Require a signal-to-noise ratio of a least _q on the first itera‐
              tion to report a valid result.  Larger values reduce the chances
              of obtaining a bogus result at the risk of obtaining  no  result
              at all.

              The default is 1.0.


       --hhsstteepp==_n
              Set  the  horizontal  increment to check every _nth column.  This
              value affects both run time and memory requirements.

              The default is 11.


       --vvsstteepp==_n
              Set the vertical increment to check every nth row.  Larger  val‐
              ues  usually work, reducing run time, but they increase the risk
              of incorrect results.

              The default is 5.


       --ddsstteepp==_n
              Set the vertical distance used when checking pixels in a column.
              The  default is intended to minimize the effect of noise along a
              horizontal boundary.

              The default is 2.


       --aasstteepp==_n
              Set the angle increment of the first iteration, in degrees.

              The default is 1.0.





RREEFFEERREENNCCEESS
       ppaammttiilltt implements a somewhat simplified algorithm inspired  by:  "Mea‐
       suring  Document  Image Skew and Orientation", by Bloomberg, Kopec, and
       Dasari.  In SPIE Volume 2422, Document Recognition II,  pages  302-316,
       February 1995.


